THERE IS NO DISCRIMINATING EVIDENCE. That is the finding, and it is about my spec.

  16 of 16 spec clauses and boundaries: all three, identically.
  Cross-examination: a full 3x3 matrix, EVERY cell passing. Each arm's suite passes against
    every other arm's implementation -- genuine mutual conformance, not the untestability that
    doctor-repo produced.
  The only critique, codex-luna on glm-53-flash: NO MATERIAL DEFECTS.
  Set semantics, which my probe initially missed: [1,1,2] is two citations, not three. All
    three return Unremarkable. I only tested that duplicates do not panic, and had to go back
    for the clause that mattered.
  The three named constants are the same three thresholds in all three, differing only in
    spelling and in whether 25% is written as a percent or a denominator of 4.

I pinned both sides of every boundary in this spec -- 3 citations against 2, 400 lines against
399, a citation exactly at 25% against 25% plus one. It worked: nobody got a boundary wrong, and
three earlier tasks were decided by exactly such disagreements. But a specification tight enough
that every competent arm converges is a specification that produces no ranking signal. The
tournament learned nothing about these three models today.

That is a real tension and it belongs on the record rather than in a bead: the tighter the spec,
the better the code and the less it discriminates. Both halves are wanted, and they pull apart.
A spec that leaves a boundary open discovers who thinks carefully; a spec that pins it gets the
boundary right. This one chose correctness, knowingly, and got it.

MERGED ON THE TIEBREAKS, which is all that was left. glm-53-flash has the most tests (44 to 43
to 38) and it is the only one of the three on a free route -- codex-luna and claude-sonnet both
draw on plans the orchestrator itself competes with. Where capability is indistinguishable, the
cheaper arm should win, and that is the whole premise of the Pareto board.

Its implementation sorts and deduplicates the citations for set semantics and widens the
comparison to u64 so the inclusive 25% boundary cannot overflow at u32::MAX. codex-luna's
critique confirmed both, and I verified them.

